Implemented Vercel Web Analytics for the chem-tutor project.

## What Was Implemented

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics following the latest official documentation from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart.

## Changes Made

### 1. Package Installation
- Installed `@vercel/analytics` version 2.0.1 using npm
- Updated package.json to include the new dependency
- Generated updated package-lock.json with the new package and its dependencies

### 2. Code Changes
Modified `/frontend/app/layout.tsx`:
- Added import statement: `import { Analytics } from "@vercel/analytics/next";`
- Added the `<Analytics />` component inside the `<body>` tag, after the existing components
- Placed it at the end of the body to follow best practices for analytics tracking

### 3. Framework-Specific Implementation
This is a Next.js App Router project, so I followed the App Router-specific instructions from the Vercel docs:
- Used the Next.js-specific import: `@vercel/analytics/next`
- Added the component to the root layout file (app/layout.tsx)
- The Analytics component is now included on all pages automatically

## Implementation Details

The Analytics component was added after the existing components (PwaServiceWorker and AuthGate) in the layout to ensure it doesn't interfere with the app's core functionality while still tracking all page views.

## Verification

1. ✅ Build completed successfully with no errors
2. ✅ Type checking passed (Next.js compilation includes TypeScript validation)
3. ✅ All files properly staged in git (app/layout.tsx, package.json, package-lock.json)

## Next Steps for Deployment

To activate Web Analytics:
1. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard (Analytics section)
2. Deploy the application to Vercel
3. Visit the deployed site to trigger analytics tracking
4. Monitor the Vercel dashboard to see analytics data appear

The implementation follows the official Vercel documentation and maintains the existing code structure without breaking any functionality.
